00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.100 align:start position:0%
this German company has been crafting  nutcrackers since 1928 they make them  out of linden wood because it's  lightweight and easy to carve to make  the nutcracker torsos workers cut a long  piece of octagonal-shaped linden into  torso sized chunks a conveyor feeds the  pieces into a multi station lathe the  first station rounds out the Octagon  then forms the basic torso shape the  second station finalizes the shape the  next four station sand the wood so that  the wood stain they'll apply later on  will penetrate evenly next the torsos go  into a vertical router three at a time  it cuts a notch in each torso for the  lever that opens and closes the  nutcrackers mouth next the torso goes to  a drilling machine the machine  simultaneously drills all the holes  required to attach the body parts they  stay in each torso a dark color  once the stain dries they spray on two  coats of semi-gloss slacker  meanwhile an automatic lathe shapes the  nutcrackers head workers attach a nose  then cut holes for the eyes they spray  on a touch of red paint to simulate a  sun-kissed nose and rosy cheeks with a  steady hand they paint the whites of the  eyes and the eyebrows once the paint  dries they apply glue to the center of  each eye then attach an iris and pupil  native enamel tin now they align the  head with the torso and screw the parts  together after decorating the coat with  buttons and a buckle they glue a strip  of rabbit fur hair to the head they also  glue on a rabbit fur beard to camouflage  the notch for the lever then they mount  the torso onto the legs which where  painted on boots and stand on a wooden  base then this Nutcracker percussionist  gets his drum along with arms and  drumsticks  they top them off with a hat made of  spray-painted wood most of the  nutcrackers parts join together with  wooden dowels a large dowel running  across the notch and the torso is the  axle on which the leper rotates
 

